1	// "Assume no toolchain on the machine that runs it", as a check on the one
2	// mechanical half of it: a script may not probe for a Swift toolchain with
3	// `command -v swift` unless `xcode-select -p` has already gated it.
4	//
5	// The owner's Mac installs the DMG from CI and has no Xcode command line tools.
6	// `command -v swift` reports success there anyway, because `/usr/bin/swift` ships
7	// on every Mac as a *stub*: run it without a developer directory and it does not
8	// fail, it pops the "install the command line tools?" panel — an 8 GB download
9	// prompted at the person diagnosing a dead microphone. The probe is therefore not
10	// a weaker test than `xcode-select -p`, it is a test of something else entirely
11	// (the stub's presence, which is universal). `xcode-select -p` fails cleanly when
12	// no developer directory is configured, so it is the gate; `command -v swift` may
